+# Decision: Concurrent Visit Edit Policy
+
+## Context
+
+A visit can be edited by more than one worker before either edit is seen by the other: tablets go offline for up to 48 hours, and workers sometimes correct the same visit independently after comparing field notes. The server receives both edits, possibly days apart and in either order, and must decide what becomes the visit's current state without treating arrival order as evidence that one worker's observations are more authoritative.
+
+A visit's scientific fields — observation date, observer list, species cover estimates, and notes — are not all independent. Some edits are unrelated (correcting an observer name, adding a missing species); others are related (revising a species identification and its cover estimate together). The server cannot reliably infer which is which from field names alone, so any mechanism that acts on fields without a coordinator's judgment risks treating a related pair as independent.
+
+## Decision Elements
+
+**Gates:**
+
+- **No silent discard** (inherited — the project tenet *do not silently discard a submitted edit*). An option that could cause a worker's submitted visit to disappear or be overwritten without a trace is eliminated.
+
+**Weighted criteria:**
+
+- **Scientific-relationship risk** (major, turns on the *keep the path from a submitted observation to an exported value inspectable* tenet) — how likely the mechanism is to combine or select among fields in a way that crosses a scientific relationship the server doesn't recognize, producing an exported value neither worker actually submitted.
+- **Review workload** (major, turns on *prefer workflows two coordinators can operate without a dedicated systems administrator*) — how much coordinator attention the policy demands per season, given two coordinators and roughly 6,000 visits.
+- **Implementation and maintenance burden** (moderate) — the ongoing cost of building and keeping correct any policy-specific logic, beyond the one-time cost of building it.
+- **Submission size and complexity** (minor) — what the tablet must compute and send, and how simple that is to implement and test offline.
+
+## Options in the Domain
+
+### Whole-visit revisions
+
+Each submission names the visit ID and the base revision ID it was edited from, and carries the complete visit payload — not a diff. The server promotes a submission to the visit's current revision when its base matches the current revision at receipt time. When it doesn't (because another submission already advanced the current revision, or two submissions share the same now-stale base), the server stores it as a competing revision and marks the visit unresolved, for a coordinator to pick one whole revision to become current.
+
+**Analysis:**
+
+- No silent discard: **passes** — every submission is retained as a revision whether or not it becomes current.
+- Scientific-relationship risk: **strong fit** — the server never acts on individual fields; a coordinator decides between whole revisions, so no automated step can cross a relationship it doesn't understand.
+- Review workload: **weak fit** — any two submissions that diverge from the same base become review work, even when they touch unrelated fields.
+- Implementation and maintenance burden: **strong fit** — the server only compares revision IDs; there is no per-field merge logic to build or keep correct as field types change.
+- Submission size and complexity: **strong fit** — the tablet sends the base revision ID and the whole payload it already holds; no diff computation.
+
+**Summary:** trades a larger review queue for zero risk of the server silently combining fields it shouldn't.
+
+### Field-aware three-way merge
+
+Each submission supplies its base state and its changes. The server combines disjoint field changes automatically; changes that touch the same field require coordinator review. This needs maintained combination rules for scalar fields, the observer list, and species entries.
+
+**Analysis:**
+
+- No silent discard: **passes** — originals are retained regardless of whether the server combines them, and automatic combination can be gated behind a requirement that every combined record still receive manual approval before export.
+- Scientific-relationship risk: **weak fit** — two edits that touch different fields can still be scientifically related (a species identification and its cover estimate); the server would combine them automatically because it has no way to see the relationship from field names.
+- Review workload: **strong fit** — only genuinely overlapping edits reach a coordinator; unrelated concurrent edits resolve themselves.
+- Implementation and maintenance burden: **weak fit** — requires combination rules per field shape (scalar, list, structured entry) that must be extended and kept correct as the visit schema evolves.
+- Submission size and complexity: **partial fit** — the tablet must retain a base snapshot and compute a structured change set rather than resend the whole visit.
+
+**Summary:** shrinks the review queue at the cost of a mechanism that can combine scientifically related edits without noticing.
+
+## Selection
+
+**Chosen: whole-visit revisions.**
+
+Both options pass the no-silent-discard gate, so the decision turns on the two major criteria. Field-aware merge wins on review workload, but whole-visit revisions wins on scientific-relationship risk — and that risk is the one the team is not willing to carry automatically: a merge rule that combines fields correctly in the common case can still cross a relationship it doesn't recognize, and when it does, the resulting exported value was never any worker's submission and the system has no coordinator judgment behind it. That failure mode runs against the *keep the path from a submitted observation to an exported value inspectable* tenet more directly than a larger review queue runs against the coordinator-workload tenet — a bigger queue is a capacity problem the team can see and staff around; a wrongly combined record is a correctness problem that may not surface until the partner questions the data. Whole-visit revisions also wins or ties on implementation burden and submission complexity, since it carries no merge rules to build or maintain.
+
+**What this forecloses:** no visit ever reaches review-export or tablet-sync already auto-combined; every multi-submission visit a coordinator sees is a whole-revision choice, not a partial merge to audit.
+
+**What this obligates downstream:**
+
+- **Tablet-sync** must retain the base revision ID alongside each local draft so a resumed or retried submission names the revision it actually started from, and must surface the server's unresolved response (and the competing-revision set) to the worker as "pending coordinator review," not as a failure or a silent no-op.
+- **Review-export** must provide a revision-comparison view over a visit's current revision and its competing revisions, record the coordinator's identity and the contributing submission IDs when one is selected (or a reconciled revision is submitted), and exclude any visit still flagged unresolved from the weekly export set.
+
+**When to revisit:** reconsider field-aware merge — likely as a narrow addition rather than a wholesale replacement — if the review queue becomes operationally unsustainable for two coordinators (sustained backlog, not an occasional busy week) *and* a merge rule can be scoped to fields narrow enough that the scientific-relationship risk is bounded (for example, observer-name corrections alone, never species or cover-estimate fields). Absent both conditions holding at once, the risk this decision was made to avoid still applies.
